messages.reasoning_content:
Type error: `shapedThread(comments, readers)` in the not-listed branch — `comments` is `Comment[]` (raw), but shapedThread needs `ThreadEntry[]` (with isHuman). I need to map first:

```ts
const entries = comments.map((comment) => ({ body: comment.body ?? "", isHuman: isHumanComment(comment) }));
return whoseTurn(shapedThread(entries, readers).events) === "agent";
```

Actually — `keptCommentIds` already does exactly this mapping internally. So in the not-listed branch I can reuse: `keptCommentIds(comments)` and ask... no — the not-listed branch needs whoseTurn over ALL listed comments, not whether a specific id is kept. Hmm, but I could compute `shapedThread` via a tiny helper. The mapping one-liner is fine. Let me restructure to share the mapping:

```ts
export function commentWouldBeRemoved(repo, number, commentId): boolean {
  const comments = listComments(repo, number);
  const listed = comments.find((comment) => String(comment.id) === String(commentId));
  const entries = comments.map((comment) => ({ body: comment.body ?? "", isHuman: isHumanComment(comment) }));
  if (listed === undefined) {
    // Read-after-write lag: ...
    return whoseTurn(shapedThread(entries, readers).events) === "agent";
  }
  return !keptCommentIds(comments).has(listed.id);
}
```

Hmm, computing `entries` eagerly even for the kept branch (where keptCommentIds recomputes it) — minor waste. Cleaner: keep the mapping inside the lag branch. Let me just inline it there.

